WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.240
Okay, so what is exactly?

00:05.240 --> 00:05.800
Functional?

00:05.800 --> 00:06.760
Why we need it now?

00:06.760 --> 00:08.960
Again, we have our data inside the table.

00:09.000 --> 00:11.560
Now there is like a lot of stuff that you can do with your data.

00:11.560 --> 00:15.920
So sometimes you have to change the values of your data, like doing data manipulation.

00:15.920 --> 00:18.840
Or you want to do some aggregations and analyses.

00:18.840 --> 00:23.480
So maybe you want to analyze your data and find insights and maybe build reports.

00:23.520 --> 00:27.840
And sometimes you might find bad data inside your tables and you want to clean that up.

00:27.840 --> 00:29.560
So you want to do data cleansing.

00:29.560 --> 00:34.960
And sometimes you have to do data transformations and data manipulation on our data in order to solve

00:34.960 --> 00:36.240
some SQL tasks.

00:36.240 --> 00:40.120
And in SQL, in order to solve those tasks, we have functions.

00:40.120 --> 00:41.880
So again, what is exactly a function?

00:41.880 --> 00:45.920
It is a built in code block that accepts an input value.

00:45.960 --> 00:51.000
Then the function is going to go and process this value and it's going to return a result an output

00:51.000 --> 00:51.440
value.

00:51.440 --> 00:55.600
So you give an input value, do some transformations and give an output.

00:55.600 --> 00:58.520
And we can group the functions into two big categories.

00:58.520 --> 01:01.280
The first one we call it single row functions.

01:01.280 --> 01:05.850
So you give the function only one value and add the return, you will get as well one value.

01:05.850 --> 01:10.850
So the input for the function going to be only one single value like Mariam, and the output of the

01:10.850 --> 01:12.090
function going to be as well.

01:12.130 --> 01:13.370
Single row value.

01:13.370 --> 01:15.290
So one value in one value out.

01:15.330 --> 01:19.530
And now the other category of functions we call it multi-row functions.

01:19.530 --> 01:25.010
So for example if you have the function sum this function accepts multiple rows multiple values like

01:25.050 --> 01:27.330
it gets 30, ten, 20, 40.

01:27.370 --> 01:32.450
The function is then going to go and summarize all those rows and return in the output only one value.

01:32.490 --> 01:35.210
The summarization of all those values can be 100.

01:35.210 --> 01:39.250
So the input is multiple rows and the output is one single value.

01:39.250 --> 01:42.410
So those are the two main categories of functions in SQL.

01:46.370 --> 01:50.890
Now my friends, you have to understand something about the functions that you can go and nest functions

01:50.890 --> 01:51.330
together.

01:51.330 --> 01:55.450
So you can use multiple functions together in order to manipulate one value.

01:55.450 --> 01:58.730
And this technique is not only in SQL in any programming language.

01:58.730 --> 01:59.850
So let's have this example.

01:59.850 --> 02:01.330
We have the function left.

02:01.330 --> 02:04.650
It can go and extract like few characters let's say two characters.

02:04.650 --> 02:07.430
So the input for this function let's say it's Maria.

02:07.470 --> 02:09.070
This value can enter the function.

02:09.070 --> 02:11.750
The function is going to go and extract the first two characters.

02:11.750 --> 02:14.270
And in the output we will get only two characters.

02:14.310 --> 02:14.710
Ma.

02:14.830 --> 02:15.990
So this is one function.

02:15.990 --> 02:17.550
We have an input and output.

02:17.590 --> 02:18.710
Now you might say you know what.

02:18.710 --> 02:20.630
We have multiple steps on this value.

02:20.630 --> 02:24.990
So the first step we want to extract the first two characters using the lift function.

02:24.990 --> 02:26.390
But we have a second step.

02:26.390 --> 02:29.990
So we want to transform this output into a lowercase characters.

02:29.990 --> 02:31.590
So we have another function lower.

02:31.590 --> 02:36.070
And the input for this second function will be the output of the first function.

02:36.070 --> 02:40.190
So ma it is at the same time output and input for another function.

02:40.190 --> 02:44.510
So the lower function can take this value and convert it into lowercase character.

02:44.510 --> 02:49.350
So it's like inside the factory the material is going to be processed into multiple stations, and the

02:49.350 --> 02:52.390
output of one station can be the input for the next station.

02:52.390 --> 02:54.630
And this is exactly what we can do with the functions.

02:54.630 --> 02:55.990
So now how are we going to build that.

02:55.990 --> 02:58.390
The first step is to start with the first function.

02:58.390 --> 03:00.190
So this is simple one function.

03:00.230 --> 03:05.510
Now for the next step what you're going to do on the left side you can write lower and put the whole

03:05.510 --> 03:06.630
thing in parentheses.

03:06.630 --> 03:10.680
So now the whole thing the first function is going to be inside another function.

03:10.680 --> 03:13.280
And with that you have nested one function in another.

03:13.280 --> 03:17.320
And of course, if you need a third function like for example, the length, what you're going to do,

03:17.320 --> 03:20.280
you're going to put the whole thing again between two parentheses.

03:20.280 --> 03:23.160
So now that means the output of the lift going to go to the lower.

03:23.160 --> 03:26.000
And the output of the lower is going to go to the length.

03:26.000 --> 03:27.040
So it is very simple.

03:27.040 --> 03:31.120
And the order of the execution for this will start always in the inner function.

03:31.120 --> 03:36.080
So the lift function can be executed first and then the outside function the lower and the last function

03:36.080 --> 03:38.160
that's going to be executed is the length.

03:38.160 --> 03:42.440
This is how the nested functions works in SQL or in any programming language.

03:46.360 --> 03:49.000
Now my friends in SQL we have a lot of functions.

03:49.000 --> 03:52.240
That's why we have to group them as well into subcategories.

03:52.240 --> 03:57.040
Like if you are talking about the single row functions, we have functions for the string values and

03:57.040 --> 04:01.960
as well for the numeric, the date and time and as well functions in order to handle the nulls.

04:01.960 --> 04:05.720
And if you are talking about the Multi-row functions here we have basically two groups.

04:05.720 --> 04:08.600
The first one is the simple aggregate functions.

04:08.600 --> 04:10.880
Those are the basics in order to aggregate your data.

04:10.880 --> 04:12.900
And we have another advanced one.

04:12.900 --> 04:16.860
We call it the window functions, or sometimes we call it analytical functions.

04:16.860 --> 04:21.020
So now if I'm looking to those two groups and now my friends, it is very important to understand those

04:21.020 --> 04:21.540
functions.

04:21.540 --> 04:24.780
Because using them you can do whatever you want with your data.

04:24.780 --> 04:29.860
And if I'm looking to those two groups, the single row functions, those stuff here, they are functions

04:29.860 --> 04:34.260
in order to manipulate and prepare the data for the second group.

04:34.260 --> 04:38.700
So if you are thinking about data engineers and data analysts, the data engineer is going to go and

04:38.700 --> 04:41.900
prepare the data in SQL using the single row functions.

04:41.900 --> 04:47.020
So you're going to use them in order to clean up, transform, manipulate your data in order to prepare

04:47.060 --> 04:48.620
it for the analysis.

04:48.620 --> 04:53.980
And if you are a data analyst, you will be mostly using the aggregate functions in almost every task.

04:53.980 --> 04:55.180
So I really see it like this.

04:55.180 --> 04:59.620
The single row functions for that engineers and multi-row functions for data analysts.

04:59.620 --> 05:00.180
And my friends.

05:00.180 --> 05:01.420
What are we going to do in this course?

05:01.420 --> 05:07.460
We're going to visit each of those subgroups one by one, exploring the functions, understanding how

05:07.460 --> 05:09.140
they work and when we're going to use them.

05:09.140 --> 05:11.940
So let's start with the first group, the string functions.

05:11.940 --> 05:15.220
And here we're going to learn how to manipulate the string values.

05:15.220 --> 05:15.940
So let's go.
